% ============================================================================
% shape-composition — how many triangles a named quadrilateral decomposes
% into, DERIVED (not looked up) from two cited geometric primitives
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY). CCSS K.G.B.6 "compose simple shapes to form
% larger shapes."
% ============================================================================
% Every other library in this directory is a `table`: one source states the
% answer directly, and recall is a plain lookup. This one is different — no
% single source states "a square is made of two triangles" as a citable
% sentence (checked MathWorld's Hexagon, Diagonal, Square, and
% IsoscelesRightTriangle pages; none of them phrase it that way, since a
% formal mathematics reference does not bother spelling out something this
% elementary). So instead of blocking on a missing verbatim quote, this
% library DERIVES the fact with a `rule`, composing two things that ARE
% independently, honestly citable:
%
%   1. `quadrilateral_property($Shape, $Property)` — the already-shipped
%      `quadrilateral-types.adj` table: square, rectangle, rhombus,
%      parallelogram, and trapezoid are each grounded, MathWorld-cited
%      quadrilaterals (4-sided polygons).
%   2. The general definitions of TRIANGULATION ("the division of a...
%      polygon into a set of triangles") and a POLYGON DIAGONAL ("a line
%      segment connecting two nonadjacent polygon vertices"). These live on
%      DIFFERENT MathWorld pages, so they are cited separately below: the
%      triangulation sentence as the rule's `source`, the diagonal sentence
%      as a `cites` carrying its own locator.
%
% Provenance, and what installment 4i had to repair. This paragraph used to
% say the rule's `source` "states plainly which part is the verbatim
% MathWorld quote and which part is the elementary reasoning step" — and the
% field marked nothing at all. It welded THREE sentences into one quotation
% under one locator, and only the first belonged there:
%
% (a) the TRIANGULATION sentence is a real span of the cited page — one
% match, and the only one of the three that was ever correctly placed. It is
% verbatim under whitespace collapse and NOT byte-exact even against the
% page's tag-stripped text, because MathWorld wraps it across a newline;
% that is the weaker claim installment 4h learned to state rather than blur.
%
% (b) the DIAGONAL sentence had ONE WORD CHANGED inside quotation marks —
% the page says "A polygon diagonal is", the field said "A polygonal
% diagonal is" — and it is prose from a DIFFERENT PAGE
% (PolygonDiagonal.html) than the locator named. It is now a `cites` in the
% page's own words with its own locator, and in that form it IS byte-exact
% against THE PAGE'S TAG-STRIPPED TEXT, which is what this package's
% contiguity contract has always measured. (Against literal raw HTML neither
% sentence is byte-exact — both are broken by `<a>` tags — and both appear
% byte-exact inside the page's `<meta>` description attributes. Saying which
% text is meant costs one clause and this effort's whole subject is claim
% strength.) Naming the right page decides nothing about #14111's
% canonical-vs-fetchable question: the sentence is simply on the page it is
% on.
%
% (c) the QUADRILATERAL sentence appears on NEITHER page. It is this rule's
% own conclusion — a quadrilateral has exactly one diagonal per
% opposite-vertex pair, and drawing it splits the 4-sided region into
% exactly two 3-sided regions — and it was presented as though quoted. It is
% no longer inside the quotation; it is stated here, as the derivation the
% `trust consensus` tier already existed to mark.
%
% So the inference is auditable end to end: a query's `steps` trail shows
% BOTH the rule's citation (the general triangulation/diagonal definitions)
% AND the underlying table fact's citation (the quadrilateral's own defining
% property), and a human can independently check each link. `trust
% consensus`, not `authoritative`, because the CONCLUSION (exactly two
% triangles) is this library's own derivation from the quoted material, not
% a sentence any single source states outright — the honest tier for
% "well-established, but assembled here rather than copied."
%
%     import "shape-composition.adj"       % transitively imports quadrilateral-types.adj
%     ? triangle_decomposition_count(square, $N)   % 2, with the full 2-step audit trail
%
% This deliberately covers only the QUADRILATERAL-by-diagonal case — the
% single most common K-2 example (splitting a square/rectangle into two
% triangles) — not a general n-sided-polygon formula. A general
% `triangles(sides) = sides - 2` rule would need its own honestly-sourced
% derivation (MathWorld's `PolygonDiagonal` page states a convex n-gon needs
% C_(n-3) triangulating diagonals but does not spell out the resulting
% n-2 triangle count), left for a future pass rather than overreaching here.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

import "quadrilateral-types.adj"

rule { head: triangle_decomposition_count($Shape, 2)
       when: quadrilateral_property($Shape, $Property)
       source "Triangulation is the division of a surface or plane polygon into a set of triangles, usually with the restriction that each triangle side is entirely shared by two adjacent triangles."
       locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Triangulation.html"
       trust consensus
       cites "A polygon diagonal is a line segment connecting two nonadjacent polygon vertices of a polygon." locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/PolygonDiagonal.html" }
